<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Lucca</h1><p class="st-meta">1160 CE – 1805 CE (approx.) · capital Lucca</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A silk-weaving Tuscan city-republic that kept its independence behind its great walls for over six centuries, until Napoleon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-lucca">Lucca</a> had been the capital of the march of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Tuscany</a>, and a commune of consuls emerged in the late 11th century. In 1160 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welf-vi">Welf VI</a> granted the city wide rights, confirming its self-government over the surrounding district.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-lucca">Lucca</a> prospered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> weaving and banking, and venerated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volto-santo-of-lucca">Volto Santo</a> crucifix. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castruccio-castracani">Castruccio Castracani</a> made himself lord from 1316 to 1328 and won a large state, but after his death the city was sold among foreign lords until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> restored its freedom in 1369. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paolo-guinigi">Paolo Guinigi</a> ruled as lord from 1400 to 1430. Afterwards an oligarchy of merchant families governed, building the massive walls of the 16th and 17th centuries.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The French occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-lucca">Lucca</a> in 1799, and in 1805 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> replaced the republic with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-lucca-and-piombino">Principality of Lucca and Piombino</a> for his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisa-bonaparte">Elisa</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-lucca.jpg" alt="Republic of Lucca" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Ninane · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lucca.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1160 CE – 1805 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lucca</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Castruccio Castracani, Paolo Guinigi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tuscan Italian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lucca, Most Serene Republic of Lucca</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1482&amp;focus=republic-of-lucca" aria-label="Republic of Lucca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4932 624 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Lucca lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5252 880L5229 694M5252 880L5250 884M5252 880L5054 728M5252 880L5252 880"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5250 884h0M5054 728h0M5252 880h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5252 880h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5252 880h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lucca. <a href="/atlas?year=1482&amp;focus=republic-of-lucca">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
